{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Proxy Pattern.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Proxy Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"proxy pattern proxy pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Proxy Pattern.md","fileName":"Proxy Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Proxy Pattern","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-26 22:56\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Object-Oriented Design Patterns]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Object-Oriented Design Patterns","Adapter Pattern","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Proxy Pattern.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Proxy Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"proxy pattern proxy pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Proxy Pattern.md","fileName":"Proxy Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Proxy Pattern","heading":"Proxy Pattern","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The Proxy pattern places a stand-in object in front of another object and preserves a compatible access surface. The proxy controls when or how the real subject is reached, such as enforcing authorization, delaying an expensive operation, or isolating a third-party component.\n\nUnlike an [[Adapter Pattern|adapter]], a proxy normally preserves the subject's conceptual interface rather than translating to a different one. Its added control should remain visible enough that callers understand latency, security, or lifetime effects.\n\nIts proxy example places a representative object in front of another object to control access or add behavior without changing the caller's interface, a structure useful for remote, protected, or deferred interactions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Object-Oriented Design Patterns","Adapter Pattern","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf"],"properties":[],"pa